<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bokar Biro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Almamy Bokar Biro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bokar Biro was the last independent almamy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Futa Jallon in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, who took power in the 1890s amid feuds between the ruling houses. His resistance to French control brought a French military expedition, which defeated and killed him at the Battle of Porédaka in November 1896. Futa Jallon was then made a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> and absorbed into French Guinea.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Futa Jallon had been founded as a theocratic state in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> launched in 1725 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamokho-alfa">Karamokho Alfa</a> and consolidated by his successor Ibrahim Sori. It was a loose federation of nine provinces, each under its own chief. Two factions, the Alfaya and the Soriya, backed the descendants of the first two rulers, and the office of almami, the head of state, passed alternately between their candidates. Bokar Biro belonged to the Soriya faction, and his base was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbo">Timbo</a>, the capital of the federation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the long reign of Almami Ibrahima Sori Dongolfella ended with his death in 1890, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elders-carthage">Council of Elders</a> chose Bokar Biro's elder brother. Bokar Biro seized power in a coup after assassinating his brother and placed loyal men in office. He faced quarrels between the factions, demands for autonomy from the rulers of Labé, Timbi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fugumba">Fugumba</a>, and the flight of slaves and free people to French-controlled zones. In July 1892 he had to cede power to Amadu of the Alfaya, and he returned in June 1894.</p><p>On 13 December 1895 rebel chiefs led by Modi Abdoullaye Dhokhiré defeated him at Bantignel, and he narrowly escaped. He reappeared weeks later at Keebu, raised an army of 1,500 soldiers and beat his enemies on 2 February 1896. The French envoy Raoul de Beeckman reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbo">Timbo</a> with troops on 18 March 1896 and demanded roads, a resident, a say over the appointment of chiefs and a trade monopoly. Bokar Biro pretended to sign a treaty, but in place of his signature he had written Bismillah, meaning in the name of God. When his term ended in April 1896 he refused to give up office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>With Futa Jallon close to civil war, the French resolved to break it into client states under their ally Umar Bademba. Columns from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> converged on the country and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbo">Timbo</a> on 3 November 1896. Bokar Biro could not rally the chiefs, and on 13 November his army was destroyed by French artillery on the plain of Porédaka; his son died with him. In June 1897 Ernest Noirot became administrator and began a programme to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. The French removed the power of the chiefs in 1904 and exiled Bokar Biro's rival Alfa Yaya in 1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bokar Biro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1896 French troops defeated and killed <b>Almamy Bokar Biro</b> at Poredaka, and Futa Jallon was absorbed into French Guinea.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-futa-jallon">Imamate of Futa Jallon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Porédaka, in what is now <a href="/countries/guinea">Guinea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.73,-12.07&amp;year=1886" aria-label="Porédaka on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4218 1763 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Bokar Biro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4668 2033h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4668 2033h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=10.73,-12.07&amp;year=1886">What was here in 1886? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
